#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079347} :activity/id #uuid "4444e6ba-d4c6-5114-a13b-7c234ea44f02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048631 :topic/id #uuid "b02557c9-cbdf-5fb2-9691-b62cc19615d1" :topic/math-academy-id 642} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079707 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll introduce a powerful technique for proving mathematical results, known as **proof by mathematical induction** (or **proof by induction** for short).\n\nMathematical induction is used to prove statements of the form\n\n$$\\forall n,\\: P(n)$$ where the universal set is $\\mathbb N.$\n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$P(n): \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^n i = 1+2+3+\\cdots + n= \\dfrac12 n (n+1)$$\n\nIt can be shown (and we'll prove it shortly) that this statement is true *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nTo get a feel for this result, let's begin by checking the first few instances:\n\n- The statement is true for $n={\\color{blue}{1}}{:}$ $$P({\\color{blue}{1}}):\\quad \\sum_{i=1}^{{\\color{blue}{1}}}i = 1 = \\dfrac12 \\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}{1}}+1)= 1\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$$\n- The statement is also true for $n={\\color{blue}{2}}{:}$ $$P({\\color{blue}{2}}):\\quad \\sum_{i=1}^{{\\color{blue}{2}}}i = 1+2 = \\dfrac12 \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}{2}}+1)= 3\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$$\n- The statement is also true for $n={\\color{blue}{3}}{:}$ $$P({\\color{blue}{3}}):\\quad \\sum_{i=1}^{{\\color{blue}{3}}}i = 1+2+3 = \\dfrac12 \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}{3}}+1) = 6\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$$\n\nUnfortunately, checking a handful of cases is not a valid proof that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n.$\n\nMoreover, if we were able to create a computer program to check that $P(n)$ is true for all values of $n$ up to, say, $10^{100},$ this *still* would not be a valid proof that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n.$\n\nTo resolve this difficulty, we can use proof by induction. To fully understand how proof by induction works, we first need to understand the **principle of induction**, which we'll discuss next." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5ed5376-35d1-532d-ae20-28f1156395c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7931 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e8034c07-39a0-5f4d-84d1-ba845b6f472c" :step/math-academy-id 181045 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079732 :tutorial/content "The **principle of mathematical induction** states the following:\n\n*Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots,$ where*\n\n- *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n- *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\n*Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\nTo understand this principle, we can view our statements as a series of dominoes, where the first domino corresponds to $P(1),$ the second domino corresponds to $P(2),$ and so on.\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7793ea71c23013f36c588634a9b55612f4eaa6c1de544ba0b9d16af0f0363.png)\n\nA typical proof using the principle of induction can be split into the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1: The Base Case**. We start by showing that the statement $P(1)$ is true.\n\n![](images/7e/7e9a144923c152243ef59660c473a69ec0452dc2fc55e8c88766da4f6a1b57a6.png)\n\n Visually, this means we're knocking down the first domino.\n- **Step 2: Inductive Hypothesis**. Here, we *assume* that the statement $P(k)$ is true for some $k \\geq 1.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb1300de827abda77862661c9bf21caaf2ca741b2135225655f4338cd6ce20ae.png)\n\n Visually, this means we're assuming that the $k$th domino has already fallen (as well as all the dominoes before).\n- **Step 3: Inductive Step**. Next, we must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$\n\n![](images/58/58805e2f8a72876b0a60134430638241d60c45526980db8fed619fc1ebe60149.png)\n\n In other words, we need to show that if the $k$th domino has fallen, then the $(k+1)$th domino must always fall, too.\n- **Step 4: Conclusion**. We conclude that $P(n)$ is true for every $n \\in \\mathbb N.$\n\n![](images/e5/e52b799ee83f01bac5ebec4034fff674c3082b37b1a2336c50894e9aefc39771.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4332c16c-6598-54bd-b129-fc5d31b57167" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9162 :tutorial/title "The Principle of Induction"} :step/id #uuid "f76d5d6f-e5a0-50ed-8ff3-cdfdda388212" :step/math-academy-id 181046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079733 :tutorial/content "So, to prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nSo, let's outline a proof strategy (or template) for constructing a proof of the result we met earlier:\n\n$$P(n): \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n i = \\dfrac12 n (n+1)$$\n\nWe'll write down each step of our framework for this particular case.\n\n***Step 1: Base Case***: *We first prove that our statement is true for the base case* $n=1.$ $$\\begin{aligned}P(1):\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}i=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (1+1)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. To do this, we assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$ This means we replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\n***Step 2: Inductive Hypothesis***: *We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have* $$P(k): \\qquad \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^k i = \\dfrac12 k (k+1).$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n***Step 3: Inductive Step***: *We're required to prove that* $$P(k+1): \\qquad \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} i = \\dfrac12 (k+1)(k+2).$$\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ our proof is complete, and we can write our conclusion.\n\n***Step 4: Conclusion***: *Therefore, we conclude that* $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n i = \\dfrac12n(n+1)$ for all $n\\geq 1.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- For now, we're simply constructing a \"bird's-eye view\" of how proof by induction works. We haven't yet proved anything! We'll get to the details of the proof shortly.\n- The inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n- It's possible to extend the principle of induction to statements of the form $$\\forall n \\geq a,\\: P(n)$$ where $a$ and $n$ are integers. In this case, the only difference is that we must assume $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\geq a$ as our inductive hypothesis.\n\nNow that we've developed a strategy, let's apply this to another result." :tutorial/id #uuid "36713977-d4ea-5064-8681-68fc3807b54c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9163 :tutorial/title "A Template for Proving Results Using Induction"} :step/id #uuid "2f8bceda-1dcb-5bfd-b26f-21d0a02f099e" :step/math-academy-id 181047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079350}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078149 :question/id #uuid "923741ad-8b78-515f-a878-805c5d0f783e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12669"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a188d81-1b50-5fa0-a400-c6a11b2a8e1b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390036}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"} :step/id #uuid "f6cbddfc-129b-512e-a0a2-b29e2765f9ca" :step/math-academy-id 181048 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079734 :tutorial/content "We're almost ready to construct our first proof by induction. Before we do, let's quickly discuss a handy trick that's often needed in these proofs.\n\nLet $a_i$ for $i\\geq 1$ be a sequence, and let $S(k)$ denote the sum of the first $k$ terms.\n\n$$S(k) = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$S(k+1) = \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i.$$\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series by induction, we often need to express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$ This is usually very easy, as we can always write the last term of the series $S(k+1)$ on its own, as follows:\n\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i\\right) + a_{k+1}.$$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7667808a-9c77-5ce8-a8d8-17051fe9df77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9164 :tutorial/title "Rewriting Finite Series"} :step/id #uuid "c4e3f144-2a05-531b-933a-a440027392e7" :step/math-academy-id 181051 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078148 :question/id #uuid "0955b292-6110-5013-ba44-04d0d7aa0880" :question/math-academy-id "e-12667"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c498824c-aaa9-53b3-aea5-9597aff81f36" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384065}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing S(k+1) in Terms of S(k)"} :step/id #uuid "b842a631-7f91-55d4-9abd-bb72fb10198e" :step/math-academy-id 181052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079719 :tutorial/content "We now have everything we need to construct a proof by induction. So, let's prove that the following statement is true for all $n\\in\\mathbb N{:}$\n\n$$P(n): \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n i = \\dfrac12 n (n+1)$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n***Base Case***: *We first prove our statement is true for the base case* $n=1.$ *For the left-hand side, we have* $$\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}i=1,\\end{aligned}$$ *and for the right-hand side, we have* $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (1+1)=1.\\end{aligned}$$ *Thus, our statement is true for* $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n***Inductive Hypothesis***: *We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have* $$\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^k i = \\dfrac12 k(k+1).$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n***Inductive Step***: *We're required to prove that* $$\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} i = \\dfrac12 (k+1)(k+2). \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows:\n\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i\\right) + a_{k+1.}$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\n*We write* $$\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}i=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}i)+(k+1).\\end{aligned}$$ *By the inductive hypothesis, we have* $$\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}i)+(k+1) & =\\frac{1}{2}k(k+1)+(k+1) \\\\ & =(k+1)(\\frac{1}{2}k+1) \\\\ & =(k+1)(\\frac{k+2}{2}) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}(k+1)(k+2),\\end{aligned}$$ *as required.*\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n*Therefore, by the principle of induction,* $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^ni = \\dfrac12 n(n+1)$ *is true for all* $n\\geq 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75853fe0-c4a5-57e5-94a5-94be43974789" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8266 :tutorial/title "Proof By Induction"} :step/id #uuid "75baef3a-99d6-571c-9a12-43df58a6000d" :step/math-academy-id 181055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079735 :tutorial/content "When writing a formal mathematical proof, we usually include only the essential details and exclude any additional commentary.\n\nSo, when communicating the full proof that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n i = \\dfrac12 n (n+1)$ is true for all $n\\in\\mathbb N,$ we could write our proof as follows:\n\n*We proceed using induction on $n.$*\n\n***Base Case***: *We first prove our statement is true for the base case* $n=1.$ *For the left-hand side, we have* $$\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}i=1,\\end{aligned}$$ *and for the right-hand side, we have* $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (1+1)=1.\\end{aligned}$$ *Thus, our statement is true for* $n=1.$\n\n***Inductive Hypothesis***: *We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have* $$\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^k i = \\dfrac12 k(k+1).$$ ***Inductive Step***: *We're required to prove that* $$\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} i = \\dfrac12 (k+1)(k+2).$$ *We write* $$\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}i & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}i)+(k+1).\\end{aligned}$$ *By the inductive hypothesis, we have* $$\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}i)+(k+1) & =\\frac{1}{2}k(k+1)+(k+1) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}(k+1)(k+2),\\end{aligned}$$ *as required.*\n\n*Therefore, by the principle of induction,* $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^ni = \\dfrac12 n(n+1)$ *is true for all* $n\\geq 1.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- For clarity, it's a good idea to state the proof method we plan to use at the beginning of the proof. For induction arguments, one possibility is to state, \"*We proceed using induction on $n$*\" at the beginning of the proof. This tells the reader that induction is the method of proof we're employing.\n- Notice that we skipped a few steps in the algebra when applying the inductive hypothesis. This is quite normal. Formal proofs usually only include the steps essential to understanding the core argument. Although, this depends on the author and the intended reader. If the intended readers are new to mathematical proofs, keeping more detail is probably a good idea." :tutorial/id #uuid "389314c0-b2e0-52ac-872b-f455d11ffb21" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9165 :tutorial/title "Stating the Full Proof"} :step/id #uuid "55eeb653-ad45-574d-ad9c-acff27ef0f9a" :step/math-academy-id 181056 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078147 :question/id #uuid "175a3e1e-2f85-555b-a0df-17e9d71f1f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12666"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56586d37-4c02-54fa-9dfc-123cef272b2d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050827}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384993}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Sums of Series Using Induction"} :step/id #uuid "04c66905-38af-5a61-a732-514cb1c85efa" :step/math-academy-id 181057}] :activity/title "Mathematical Induction" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078664}]}
